sections.s2_technical:
The mechanics of native serialization to a byte array are straightforward. You create a ByteArrayOutputStream as the sink, wrap it in an ObjectOutputStream, and call writeObject:

```java
ByteArrayOutputStream buffer = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
try (ObjectOutputStream oos = new ObjectOutputStream(buffer)) {
    oos.writeObject(account);
}
byte[] bytes = buffer.toByteArray(); // the serialized form
```

To read it back, you create a ByteArrayInputStream over those bytes, wrap it in an ObjectInputStream, and call readObject, casting to the expected type:

```java
try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes))) {
    Account restored = (Account) ois.readObject();
}
```

This works only when the class implements Serializable, every referenced class is serializable or transient, and the serialVersionUID matches.

Now the reasons schema formats win for microservices. First, cross-language reach: native serialization bytes can only be read by Java, so a Python or Go service cannot consume them, while JSON, Avro, and Protobuf have libraries in every major language. Second, version tolerance: JSON with a tolerant reader and especially Avro and Protobuf are designed so that adding a field does not break old consumers, whereas native serialization ties the bytes to the exact class and its serialVersionUID. Third, size and speed: Protobuf and Avro produce compact binary payloads that are much smaller and faster to parse than native Java serialization, which matters at banking message volumes. Fourth, and most important, security: deserializing untrusted native Java bytes can trigger gadget chains that execute arbitrary code, a class of vulnerability that has caused real breaches, so you must never call readObject on data from an untrusted source. JSON and Protobuf parsers build plain data and do not instantiate arbitrary classes from the payload, which removes that attack surface.

A senior point is where each format fits. JSON is the default for REST APIs because it is human-readable and universally supported. Protobuf is ideal for gRPC and high-throughput internal calls because it is compact and strongly schema-typed. Avro is common in Kafka pipelines because it carries schemas and supports schema evolution through a registry. Native Java serialization is acceptable only for controlled, internal, Java-only, trusted scenarios such as certain caches, and even then teams increasingly avoid it. Another senior point is that when you must accept native serialized data, you should use an ObjectInputFilter to allowlist exactly the classes permitted, which closes most of the security hole.

